All approaches to correct for or quantify bias due to selective reporting rely on overly simplistic models of bias, depend on strong assumptions that are almost always violated, and for these reasons, bias-adjusted effect size estimates rarely correspond to empirically observed effect sizes from unbiased studies [@kvarvenComparingMetaanalysesPreregistered2020]. These problems are even more pronounced when a bias-adjusted effect size estimate is calculated from a single effect size. Bias-adjusted effect size estimates that differ substantially from the unadjusted effect size should not be regarded as the actual effect size, but as a strong motivation to perform an unbiased replication study.
critical_t2s(m1 = m1, m2 = m2, sd1 = sd1, sd2 = sd2, n1 = n1, n2 = n2)
m1 <- 0.5
m2 <- 0
sd1 <- 1
sd2 <- 1
n1 <- 64
n2 <- 64
critical_t2s(m1 = m1, m2 = m2, sd1 = sd1, sd2 = sd2, n1 = n1, n2 = n2)
